m MUST reject any request to spawn a subagent that would exceed the configured delegation depth bound. | Attempting to spawn a subagent beyond the configured maximum delegation depth MUST fail closed and be rejected before subagent initialization. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:46:| ACT-003 | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | Subagent completion MUST constitute an advisory handoff to the parent agent. A subagent MUST NOT possess merge authority, worktree finalization authority, or completion authority over the parent task. | The parent agent MUST inspect subagent handoff results, incorporate candidate modifications, and submit the unified candidate to authoritative verification. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:47:| ACT-004 | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | Execution relationships MUST be explicitly classified as one of: `INTEGRATED` (shared application lifecycle and contract), `BOUNDED` (explicit adapter with declared authority/evidence boundary), or `INDEPENDENT` (deliberate separate lifecycle and fault domain). | The system MUST NOT treat an independent domain as integrated without explicit bridging contracts. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_CLOSURE_ASSESSMENT.md:108:| **Domain 04** | Actor, Domain & Delegation (`ACT`) | 4 | 4 | 0 | **CLOSED** | WP-02 |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_CLOSURE_ASSESSMENT.md:132:| **04** | Actor, Domain & Delegation | 4 | 4 | 0 | **100% SATISFIED** | Formal actor taxonomy, bounded subagent delegation depth (`ACT-001..004`). |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:23:| `ACT-001` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | The architecture MUST formally distinguish the following actor identities across all op... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:24:| `ACT-002` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent delegation MUST enforce an explicit, bounded delegation depth governed by conf... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:25:| `ACT-003` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent completion MUST constitute an advisory handoff to the parent agent. A subagent... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:26:| `ACT-004` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Execution relationships MUST be explicitly classified as one of: `INTEGRATED` (shared a... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:20:| `ACT-001` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | The architecture MUST formally distinguish the following actor identities across all op... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:21:| `ACT-002` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent delegation MUST enforce an explicit, bounded delegation depth governed by conf... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:22:| `ACT-003` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent completion MUST constitute an advisory handoff to the parent agent. A subagent... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:23:| `ACT-004` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Execution relationships MUST be explicitly classified as one of: `INTEGRATED` (shared a... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:193:## 8. Actor, Domain, and Delegation Requirements\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:205:|         | Delegation (Bounded)  |                        | Subprocess Spawn       |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:115:| **IR-10** | `HIDDEN_POLICY_DECISION` | **MINOR** | `ACT-002` | Hardcoding Subagent Delegation Depth Limit ($Depth \\le 2$) as Invariant |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:859:| `ACT-002` | MISSING | `AuthorityAdmissionEngine` (INC-03) | Delegation chain test | Tracks delegation depth & parent |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE4_IMPLEMENTATION_WORK_PACKAGES.md:1234:| `ACT-002` | ACT | MISSING | **WP-02** (INC-03) | WP-03, WP-04, WP-08 | Delegation chain test |\nBinary file origin/main:docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.14/platform-e2/lifecycle-targeted/fixtures/broken-release/download/v1.0.0-rc.14/nexus-linux-x86_64-v1.0.0-rc.14.tar.gz matches\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:1:# PA-4 baseline: delegation and workflow execution\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:8:| Transient delegation | Partially implemented; qualification pending | `runSubagent` remains the in-turn child path ([agent.ts](../../cli/src/agent.ts)); `spawn_subagent` now selects a predefined profile, narrows tools at schema and dispatcher boundaries, clears inherited full access, and emits a typed receipt ([spine_handlers.ts](../../cli/src/core/spine_handlers.ts), [dispatch_tool_call.ts](../../cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts)). Prompt-only calls default to read-only `semantic_explorer`; writable profiles require an established worktree. Depth and per-parent child-attempt limits are enforced. Process-wide resource budgets, full task/session/workspace grant derivation, deterministic security tests, and independent evidence review remain outstanding; see [transient authority milestone](PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md).\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:19:- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:30:- This is an implementation checkpoint, not completion evidence for PA-4. Authority enforcement, prompt-only runtime compatibility tests, distinct pause semantics, `/loop`, `/goal`, full batch terminal-state persistence, adversarial coverage, interface-level behavior tests, minimal-provider workflow fixtures, independent review, and comparative evaluation remain outstanding.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:1:# PA-4 transient delegation authority milestone\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:11:- A child has `fullAccess: false`; a writable profile requires an already established isolated worktree. Delegation depth is limited to one child level and each parent admits at most four child attempts.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:13:- Operators may set `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_CHILDREN` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_ACTIVE` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_STEPS` (1–256), and `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_DURATION_MS` (1,000–600,000). Invalid values fail closed. Delegation depth is fixed at one level.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:17:These restrictions preserve prompt-only compatibility while intentionally refusing legacy prompt-only requests that relied on unrestricted writes. The process-wide active-child ceiling is shared by transient delegations; aggregate budgets across separate parent agents and independent evidence review remain open PA-4 work.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:97:| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:105:PA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\n# PA-4 baseline: delegation and workflow execution\n\n**Snapshot:** Nexus `0151bef3a8a95e3cd3df2d632439b30811e6415c` (`origin/main`), inspected 2026-11-21. This is a source and test inventory; source presence and test-file names are not runtime verification, and the existing tests listed here were not run for this baseline.\n\n| Area | Status | Evidence and observed boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interactive turns and inference | Implemented | `AutonomousAgent.runTurn` delegates to `runTurnImpl` ([agent.ts:4186](../../cli/src/agent.ts#L4186), [run_turn.ts:26](../../cli/src/core/run_turn.ts#L26)); provider recovery and context admission are in the turn path. `TurnResult` distinguishes interrupted, blocked and failed executions and requires evidence for verified outcomes ([turn.ts:17](../../cli/src/core/turn.ts#L17), [turn.ts:423](../../cli/src/core/turn.ts#L423)). |\n| Transient delegation | Partially implemented; qualification pending | `runSubagent` remains the in-turn child path ([agent.ts](../../cli/src/agent.ts)); `spawn_subagent` now selects a predefined profile, narrows tools at schema and dispatcher boundaries, clears inherited full access, and emits a typed receipt ([spine_handlers.ts](../../cli/src/core/spine_handlers.ts), [dispatch_tool_call.ts](../../cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts)). Prompt-only calls default to read-only `semantic_explorer`; writable profiles require an established worktree. Depth and per-parent child-attempt limits are enforced. Process-wide resource budgets, full task/session/workspace grant derivation, deterministic security tests, and independent evidence review remain outstanding; see [transient authority milestone](PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md).\n| Durable work units and scheduling | Implemented | `newOperation` constructs operation state ([operation.ts:502](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts#L502)) and the graph rejects dependency cycles ([operation.ts:582](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts#L582)); `ConvergenceScheduler` selects transitions ([scheduler.ts:53](../../cli/src/core/scheduler.ts#L53)); `driveOperation` and `OperationStore` persist and resume work ([operation_runner.ts:717](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts#L717), [operation_runner_persistence.ts:28](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner_persistence.ts#L28)). Existing tests cover schema, restart, concurrency and isolated integration.\n| Roles and independent review | Implemented, incomplete | Specialist profiles define permitted tools, read-only declarations, inputs, ceilings and output contracts ([specialist_profiles.ts:22](../../cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts#L22)); operation roles include team and specialist profiles ([roles.ts:101](../../cli/src/core/roles.ts#L101)). Independent-review formatting and verdict validation exist ([independent_review.ts:131](../../cli/src/core/independent_review.ts#L131), [independent_review.ts:204](../../cli/src/core/independent_review.ts#L204)); source presence does not establish all requested independence dimensions or reviewer behavior under truncated evidence.\n| Resource and provider handling | Implemented, incomplete | `ResourceRegistry` handles eligibility and provider conditions ([resources.ts:194](../../cli/src/core/resources.ts#L194)); provider capability states include `unknown` and `unavailable` ([provider_capabilities.ts:26](../../cli/src/core/provider_capabilities.ts#L26)); recovery classification/controller exists ([provider_recovery.ts:96](../../cli/src/core/provider_recovery.ts#L96), [provider_recovery.ts:367](../../cli/src/core/provider_recovery.ts#L367)). Provider test sources include `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts`, `provider_capabilities.test.ts`, `provider_robustness.test.ts`, `cloud_providers.test.ts` and `provider_recovery.test.ts`; this assignment's complete workflow has not been run against the requested minimal contract fixture.\n| `/team` and operation CLI | Implemented, incomplete | `TeamOrchestrator` maps roles to durable work units ([team.ts:43](../../cli/src/team.ts#L43), [team.ts:126](../../cli/src/team.ts#L126)); the operation CLI accepts validated JSON work-unit specs ([operation_cli.ts:106](../../cli/src/operation_cli.ts#L106)). The REPL has a dedicated `/team` handler ([repl.ts:1702](../../cli/src/repl.ts#L1702)); the sidecar routes commands through a separate slash router ([router.ts:14](../../cli/src/slash/router.ts#L14)). TUI completion/help comes from the slash catalogue ([catalog.ts:138](../../cli/src/slash/catalog.ts#L138)), with remote invocation parsing in Rust ([slash.rs:25](../../core/src/bin/nexus_tui/slash.rs#L25)).\n| `/loop`, `/goal`, `/batch` | `/batch` implemented, `/loop` and `/goal` unimplemented | `/batch` is present in the command catalogue and routes to `BatchOrchestrator`; it validates existing operation JSON specs and persists through `OperationStore` and `OperationRunner` ([batch.ts](../../cli/src/batch.ts), [batch_spec.ts](../../cli/src/batch_spec.ts), [router.ts](../../cli/src/slash/router.ts), [repl.ts](../../cli/src/repl.ts)). `batch.test.ts` and `batch_spec.test.ts` cover command behavior. `/loop` and `/goal` lifecycle controllers remain absent; loop detector tests do not implement durable loop state, and the objective ledger has no `/goal` lifecycle.\n| Pause, cancellation and restart | Implemented, incomplete | Durable operations include distinct `PAUSED`, cancellation and bounded restart reconciliation ([operation.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts), [operation_runner.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts), [operation_cli.ts](../../cli/src/operation_cli.ts)). `/batch` pause is honored at a safe boundary. Unknown effects still need stronger reconciliation semantics for the broader delegation assignment; `/loop` lifecycle recovery is absent.\n| Skills and authority | Implemented, incomplete | Skill activation has constraints and content digests ([skill_activation.ts:67](../../cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts#L67), [skill_activation.ts:104](../../cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts#L104)). Capability registration exists ([capabilities.ts:120](../../cli/src/core/capabilities.ts#L120)) and the orchestration capability schema declares `spawn_subagent` ([orchestration.ts:38](../../cli/src/core/capability_definitions/orchestration.ts#L38)). Full effect-path mediation and path-scoped profile/skill eligibility still require adversarial tests from the assignment.\n\n## Roadmap and compatibility notes\n\n- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\n- Operation records are versioned and reject records from a newer schema ([operation_schema.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts)). PA-1 resource reservations use schema v4; old records remain readable and schedule serially when their prior parallel declarations lack reservations.\n- XERJ definition-first lookup identified `TeamOrchestrator`, `WorkUnitSpec`, `newOperation` and the existing dependency-cycle check. The first batch-spec package reuses the operation parser and constructor; no orchestration code was copied.\n- No provider, gateway, or homelab-ai change is part of the workstream.\n\n## Implementation progress (2026-11-21)\n\n- `/batch` uses operation specifications and the existing `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`; it defaults to sequential execution. Parallel admission requires isolated worktrees and, as of PA-1 resource-admission work, explicit per-operation CPU/memory reservations. This adds no second scheduler or persisted workflow store.\n- `/batch` is wired into the sidecar slash router and REPL, so the TUI obtains its completion/help entry from the same slash catalogue. The implemented actions are `run`, `validate`, `status`, `list`, `pause`, `resume`, `cancel`, and `results`. Pause writes the durable `PAUSED` status at a safe boundary. `/loop` and `/goal` lifecycles remain unimplemented.\n- Regression tests cover quoting/Unicode, workspace and symlink boundaries, sequential execution, durable state, partial aggregation, CPU/memory admission and legacy serial fallback. The PA-1 slice passed 44 focused operation/batch tests, 28 operation release/review tests, and the CLI TypeScript build in its isolated worktree. These are targeted results; they do not replace the prescribed full-suite qualification. No provider code changed.\n- A definition-first XERJ lookup in the `opencode` corpus found `packages/schema/src/goal.ts:11` (MIT) with bounded round count, stall count, and boot identity fields. This informed the roadmap comparison only; no peer code was copied. The corpus is flagged as incompletely indexed, and the listed `goose` corpus was unavailable.\n- This is an implementation checkpoint, not completion evidence for PA-4. Authority enforcement, prompt-only runtime compatibility tests, distinct pause semantics, `/loop`, `/goal`, full batch terminal-state persistence, adversarial coverage, interface-level behavior tests, minimal-provider workflow fixtures, independent review, and comparative evaluation remain outstanding.\n# PA-4 transient delegation authority milestone\n\nThis milestone narrows the existing in-turn `spawn_subagent` path. It does not introduce another scheduler or durable store.\n\n## Contract\n\n- The compatible prompt-only call selects `semantic_explorer`, a predefined read-only profile.\n- Optional `profile` selects only an existing role; optional `inputs` accepts string values and conveys task data, never authority.\n- Unknown fields, unknown profiles, non-string inputs, and oversized requests are rejected before child construction. The legacy `max_steps` field remains accepted but is ignored and never changes host limits.\n- Child tools are the intersection of the parent's currently offered tools, explicit matching parent grants (when configured), and the selected profile's allowlist. The dispatcher repeats this exact-name check before any tool effect.\n- A child has `fullAccess: false`; a writable profile requires an already established isolated worktree. Delegation depth is limited to one child level and each parent admits at most four child attempts.\n- Active children, delegated inference steps, and execution duration are bounded. Defaults are four active children, 64 delegated inference requests per parent turn, and two minutes per child.\n- Operators may set `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_CHILDREN` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_ACTIVE` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_STEPS` (1–256), and `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_DURATION_MS` (1,000–600,000). Invalid values fail closed. Delegation depth is fixed at one level.\n- Completion returns a validated structured receipt. An incomplete child with workspace mutations is `UNKNOWN_EFFECT`; a reported failure is not treated as completion.\n- The child uses its own conversation and returns only a bounded summary receipt to the parent. Parent cancellation continues to signal the child.\n\nThese restrictions preserve prompt-only compatibility while intentionally refusing legacy prompt-only requests that relied on unrestricted writes. The process-wide active-child ceiling is shared by transient delegations; aggregate budgets across separate parent agents and independent evidence review remain open PA-4 work.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ was consulted per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md`. The useful reference was the peer pattern of host-owned predefined role profiles with allowlisted tools. No peer implementation was copied. XERJ field report was submitted after preview; GitHub PR creation was unavailable and the report endpoint accepted the fallback.\n\n## Qualification status\n\nThe shared `SUITE` claim was active during implementation, so no local tests were run for this milestone. TypeScript compilation and hosted CI are required before merge. This document records implementation intent, not test evidence.\n# `/batch` on the operation engine\n\n`/batch` accepts the same JSON work-unit format as `nexus operation run`. It validates the whole dependency graph before starting, then stores and executes it through `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"docs-and-tests\",\n  \"objective\": \"Update the guide and verify the examples\",\n  \"units\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"guide\",\n      \"title\": \"Update the guide\",\n      \"role\": \"implementer\",\n      \"prompt\": \"Update the guide examples for the new API\",\n      \"authorizedScope\": [\"docs/guide.md\"],\n      \"executionResources\": { \"cpuMilli\": 1000, \"memoryBytes\": 536870912 }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"verify\",\n      \"title\": \"Verify the examples\",\n      \"role\": \"tester\",\n      \"prompt\": \"Run the documented examples and report the results\",\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"guide\"],\n      \"executionResources\": { \"cpuMilli\": 1000, \"memoryBytes\": 536870912 }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe accepted fields are the operation fields: `objective`, optional `id`, `units`, optional `resources`, `discoverCatalog`, `policy`, `git`, and `boundaries`. A unit uses `prompt` for its task, optional `role`, `dependsOn`, `requires`, `required`, `taskClass`, `contextBudget`, `inputs`, `authorizedScope`, `unresolvedAssumptions`, and optional `executionResources`. Unknown keys, missing dependencies, and cycles are refused before execution.\n\n## Commands\n\n```text\n/batch <spec-file>\n/batch run <spec-file>\n/batch validate <spec-file>\n/batch status [id]\n/batch list\n/batch pause [id]\n/batch resume [id]\n/batch cancel [id]\n/batch results [id]\n```\n\nPaths may be quoted or backslash-escaped and must resolve to a regular file inside the selected workspace. The file is limited to 256 KiB. `run` is sequential by default. To request parallel units, set `policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `policy.executionBudget`, `git.isolate: true`, and `executionResources` on every unit. `cpuMilli` is a reservation in thousandths of a CPU core; `memoryBytes` is a byte reservation. The scheduler admits a unit only when its declared reservation fits the operation budget, alongside model/resource eligibility, dependencies, provider slot limits, and serialized verified integration. These declarations are admission estimates supplied by the operator; Nexus does not enforce OS-level CPU or memory quotas and does not report them as measured usage. Without complete reservations or isolated worktrees, new parallel specs are refused, and older persisted parallel records run serially.\n\nWhen an ID is omitted, a single active batch is selected. Multiple active batches require an explicit ID; if none is active, a sole batch is selected only when that is unambiguous. `/batch list` shows the available IDs. `status` shows progress; `results` shows each task's status, summary, and recorded verifier state. A blocked required task prevents `COMPLETE`; aggregate output reports `PARTIAL`, `BLOCKED`, `FAILED`, or `CANCELLED` as derived from the durable operation record.\n\nThe operation scheduler continues independent work while dependencies remain blocked. Retry, provider recovery, and restart use the existing operation decisions and state. Cancellation of an active `/batch run` uses the same signal as `/team`; a separate cancel request refuses an unsettled stored unit until recovery can classify it.\n\n## Current implementation boundary\n\nPause writes a distinct `PAUSED` status into the durable operation record under the operation driver lock. If a work unit is currently executing, pause returns a safe-boundary message; retry once the unit settles. Resume continues from the preserved record. Schema v4 adds explicit execution reservations; older records migrate additively and missing reservations clamp parallel scheduling to one. `/loop`, `/goal`, independent acceptance review, and minimal-provider end-to-end fixtures are not supplied by this batch slice.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":220607660}},"started_at_ms":1795297713264,"completed_at_ms":1795297713485}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-21T21:48:33.627Z","ordinal":35629,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a20015-1ddb-70a2-999b-37105c3c8cd4","call_id":"call_ba1418a67eae4d709e8be9559992e61c","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"origin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_ASSESSMENT.md:159:| 04 Actor, Domain & Delegation | 4 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | SATISFIED |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:44:| ACT-001 | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | The architecture MUST formally distinguish the following actor identities across all operations: `Human Operator`, `Primary Agent`, `Subagent`, `Execution Subprocess`, `Inference Provider`, `Extension/Plugin`, and `Independent Evaluator`. | Every logged event, tool execution, and state change MUST be attributed to an explicit actor from this taxonomy. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:45:| ACT-002 | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | Subagent delegation MUST enforce an explicit, bounded delegation depth governed by configured execution policy (with a recommended default bound of $Depth \\le 2$). The system MUST reject any request to spawn a subagent that would exceed the configured delegation depth bound. | Attempting to spawn a subagent beyond the configured maximum delegation depth MUST fail closed and be rejected before subagent initialization. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:46:| ACT-003 | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | Subagent completion MUST constitute an advisory handoff to the parent agent. A subagent MUST NOT possess merge authority, worktree finalization authority, or completion authority over the parent task. | The parent agent MUST inspect subagent handoff results, incorporate candidate modifications, and submit the unified candidate to authoritative verification. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_ACCEPTANCE_MATRIX.md:47:| ACT-004 | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | Execution relationships MUST be explicitly classified as one of: `INTEGRATED` (shared application lifecycle and contract), `BOUNDED` (explicit adapter with declared authority/evidence boundary), or `INDEPENDENT` (deliberate separate lifecycle and fault domain). | The system MUST NOT treat an independent domain as integrated without explicit bridging contracts. | Phase 2 §8 | `cli/src/core/authority.ts`; cli/test/authority.test.ts | DEMONSTRATED | None | Satisfied |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_FINAL_CLOSURE_ASSESSMENT.md:108:| **Domain 04** | Actor, Domain & Delegation (`ACT`) | 4 | 4 | 0 | **CLOSED** | WP-02 |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_CLOSURE_ASSESSMENT.md:132:| **04** | Actor, Domain & Delegation | 4 | 4 | 0 | **100% SATISFIED** | Formal actor taxonomy, bounded subagent delegation depth (`ACT-001..004`). |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:23:| `ACT-001` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | The architecture MUST formally distinguish the following actor identities across all op... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:24:| `ACT-002` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent delegation MUST enforce an explicit, bounded delegation depth governed by conf... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:25:| `ACT-003` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent completion MUST constitute an advisory handoff to the parent agent. A subagent... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_POST_WP05_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:26:| `ACT-004` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Execution relationships MUST be explicitly classified as one of: `INTEGRATED` (shared a... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:20:| `ACT-001` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | The architecture MUST formally distinguish the following actor identities across all op... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:21:| `ACT-002` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent delegation MUST enforce an explicit, bounded delegation depth governed by conf... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:22:| `ACT-003` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Subagent completion MUST constitute an advisory handoff to the parent agent. A subagent... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/ISSUE12_REQUIREMENT_CLOSURE_MATRIX.md:23:| `ACT-004` | 04: Actor, Domain & Delegation | WP-02 (INC-03) | Execution relationships MUST be explicitly classified as one of: `INTEGRATED` (shared a... | `cli/src/core/authority.ts` | cli/test/authority.test.ts | **DEMONSTRATED** | None |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:193:## 8. Actor, Domain, and Delegation Requirements\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:205:|         | Delegation (Bounded)  |                        | Subprocess Spawn       |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE2_REQUIREMENTS_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW.md:115:| **IR-10** | `HIDDEN_POLICY_DECISION` | **MINOR** | `ACT-002` | Hardcoding Subagent Delegation Depth Limit ($Depth \\le 2$) as Invariant |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE3_IMPLEMENTATION_DECOMPOSITION.md:859:| `ACT-002` | MISSING | `AuthorityAdmissionEngine` (INC-03) | Delegation chain test | Tracks delegation depth & parent |\norigin/main:docs/PHASE4_IMPLEMENTATION_WORK_PACKAGES.md:1234:| `ACT-002` | ACT | MISSING | **WP-02** (INC-03) | WP-03, WP-04, WP-08 | Delegation chain test |\nBinary file origin/main:docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.14/platform-e2/lifecycle-targeted/fixtures/broken-release/download/v1.0.0-rc.14/nexus-linux-x86_64-v1.0.0-rc.14.tar.gz matches\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:1:# PA-4 baseline: delegation and workflow execution\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:8:| Transient delegation | Partially implemented; qualification pending | `runSubagent` remains the in-turn child path ([agent.ts](../../cli/src/agent.ts)); `spawn_subagent` now selects a predefined profile, narrows tools at schema and dispatcher boundaries, clears inherited full access, and emits a typed receipt ([spine_handlers.ts](../../cli/src/core/spine_handlers.ts), [dispatch_tool_call.ts](../../cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts)). Prompt-only calls default to read-only `semantic_explorer`; writable profiles require an established worktree. Depth and per-parent child-attempt limits are enforced. Process-wide resource budgets, full task/session/workspace grant derivation, deterministic security tests, and independent evidence review remain outstanding; see [transient authority milestone](PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md).\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:19:- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_BASELINE.md:30:- This is an implementation checkpoint, not completion evidence for PA-4. Authority enforcement, prompt-only runtime compatibility tests, distinct pause semantics, `/loop`, `/goal`, full batch terminal-state persistence, adversarial coverage, interface-level behavior tests, minimal-provider workflow fixtures, independent review, and comparative evaluation remain outstanding.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:1:# PA-4 transient delegation authority milestone\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:11:- A child has `fullAccess: false`; a writable profile requires an already established isolated worktree. Delegation depth is limited to one child level and each parent admits at most four child attempts.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:13:- Operators may set `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_CHILDREN` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_ACTIVE` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_STEPS` (1–256), and `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_DURATION_MS` (1,000–600,000). Invalid values fail closed. Delegation depth is fixed at one level.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md:17:These restrictions preserve prompt-only compatibility while intentionally refusing legacy prompt-only requests that relied on unrestricted writes. The process-wide active-child ceiling is shared by transient delegations; aggregate budgets across separate parent agents and independent evidence review remain open PA-4 work.\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:97:| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\norigin/main:docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:105:PA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\n# PA-4 baseline: delegation and workflow execution\n\n**Snapshot:** Nexus `0151bef3a8a95e3cd3df2d632439b30811e6415c` (`origin/main`), inspected 2026-11-21. This is a source and test inventory; source presence and test-file names are not runtime verification, and the existing tests listed here were not run for this baseline.\n\n| Area | Status | Evidence and observed boundary |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interactive turns and inference | Implemented | `AutonomousAgent.runTurn` delegates to `runTurnImpl` ([agent.ts:4186](../../cli/src/agent.ts#L4186), [run_turn.ts:26](../../cli/src/core/run_turn.ts#L26)); provider recovery and context admission are in the turn path. `TurnResult` distinguishes interrupted, blocked and failed executions and requires evidence for verified outcomes ([turn.ts:17](../../cli/src/core/turn.ts#L17), [turn.ts:423](../../cli/src/core/turn.ts#L423)). |\n| Transient delegation | Partially implemented; qualification pending | `runSubagent` remains the in-turn child path ([agent.ts](../../cli/src/agent.ts)); `spawn_subagent` now selects a predefined profile, narrows tools at schema and dispatcher boundaries, clears inherited full access, and emits a typed receipt ([spine_handlers.ts](../../cli/src/core/spine_handlers.ts), [dispatch_tool_call.ts](../../cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts)). Prompt-only calls default to read-only `semantic_explorer`; writable profiles require an established worktree. Depth and per-parent child-attempt limits are enforced. Process-wide resource budgets, full task/session/workspace grant derivation, deterministic security tests, and independent evidence review remain outstanding; see [transient authority milestone](PA4_TRANSIENT_AUTHORITY.md).\n| Durable work units and scheduling | Implemented | `newOperation` constructs operation state ([operation.ts:502](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts#L502)) and the graph rejects dependency cycles ([operation.ts:582](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts#L582)); `ConvergenceScheduler` selects transitions ([scheduler.ts:53](../../cli/src/core/scheduler.ts#L53)); `driveOperation` and `OperationStore` persist and resume work ([operation_runner.ts:717](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts#L717), [operation_runner_persistence.ts:28](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner_persistence.ts#L28)). Existing tests cover schema, restart, concurrency and isolated integration.\n| Roles and independent review | Implemented, incomplete | Specialist profiles define permitted tools, read-only declarations, inputs, ceilings and output contracts ([specialist_profiles.ts:22](../../cli/src/core/specialist_profiles.ts#L22)); operation roles include team and specialist profiles ([roles.ts:101](../../cli/src/core/roles.ts#L101)). Independent-review formatting and verdict validation exist ([independent_review.ts:131](../../cli/src/core/independent_review.ts#L131), [independent_review.ts:204](../../cli/src/core/independent_review.ts#L204)); source presence does not establish all requested independence dimensions or reviewer behavior under truncated evidence.\n| Resource and provider handling | Implemented, incomplete | `ResourceRegistry` handles eligibility and provider conditions ([resources.ts:194](../../cli/src/core/resources.ts#L194)); provider capability states include `unknown` and `unavailable` ([provider_capabilities.ts:26](../../cli/src/core/provider_capabilities.ts#L26)); recovery classification/controller exists ([provider_recovery.ts:96](../../cli/src/core/provider_recovery.ts#L96), [provider_recovery.ts:367](../../cli/src/core/provider_recovery.ts#L367)). Provider test sources include `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts`, `provider_capabilities.test.ts`, `provider_robustness.test.ts`, `cloud_providers.test.ts` and `provider_recovery.test.ts`; this assignment's complete workflow has not been run against the requested minimal contract fixture.\n| `/team` and operation CLI | Implemented, incomplete | `TeamOrchestrator` maps roles to durable work units ([team.ts:43](../../cli/src/team.ts#L43), [team.ts:126](../../cli/src/team.ts#L126)); the operation CLI accepts validated JSON work-unit specs ([operation_cli.ts:106](../../cli/src/operation_cli.ts#L106)). The REPL has a dedicated `/team` handler ([repl.ts:1702](../../cli/src/repl.ts#L1702)); the sidecar routes commands through a separate slash router ([router.ts:14](../../cli/src/slash/router.ts#L14)). TUI completion/help comes from the slash catalogue ([catalog.ts:138](../../cli/src/slash/catalog.ts#L138)), with remote invocation parsing in Rust ([slash.rs:25](../../core/src/bin/nexus_tui/slash.rs#L25)).\n| `/loop`, `/goal`, `/batch` | `/batch` implemented, `/loop` and `/goal` unimplemented | `/batch` is present in the command catalogue and routes to `BatchOrchestrator`; it validates existing operation JSON specs and persists through `OperationStore` and `OperationRunner` ([batch.ts](../../cli/src/batch.ts), [batch_spec.ts](../../cli/src/batch_spec.ts), [router.ts](../../cli/src/slash/router.ts), [repl.ts](../../cli/src/repl.ts)). `batch.test.ts` and `batch_spec.test.ts` cover command behavior. `/loop` and `/goal` lifecycle controllers remain absent; loop detector tests do not implement durable loop state, and the objective ledger has no `/goal` lifecycle.\n| Pause, cancellation and restart | Implemented, incomplete | Durable operations include distinct `PAUSED`, cancellation and bounded restart reconciliation ([operation.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation.ts), [operation_runner.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts), [operation_cli.ts](../../cli/src/operation_cli.ts)). `/batch` pause is honored at a safe boundary. Unknown effects still need stronger reconciliation semantics for the broader delegation assignment; `/loop` lifecycle recovery is absent.\n| Skills and authority | Implemented, incomplete | Skill activation has constraints and content digests ([skill_activation.ts:67](../../cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts#L67), [skill_activation.ts:104](../../cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts#L104)). Capability registration exists ([capabilities.ts:120](../../cli/src/core/capabilities.ts#L120)) and the orchestration capability schema declares `spawn_subagent` ([orchestration.ts:38](../../cli/src/core/capability_definitions/orchestration.ts#L38)). Full effect-path mediation and path-scoped profile/skill eligibility still require adversarial tests from the assignment.\n\n## Roadmap and compatibility notes\n\n- v1.3 PA-1, PA-2 and PA-3 already cover parallel visibility, verified integration and goal semantics. PA-4 stages this assignment against those units and the active WP-P10-02 durable-budget work.\n- Operation records are versioned and reject records from a newer schema ([operation_schema.ts](../../cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts)). PA-1 resource reservations use schema v4; old records remain readable and schedule serially when their prior parallel declarations lack reservations.\n- XERJ definition-first lookup identified `TeamOrchestrator`, `WorkUnitSpec`, `newOperation` and the existing dependency-cycle check. The first batch-spec package reuses the operation parser and constructor; no orchestration code was copied.\n- No provider, gateway, or homelab-ai change is part of the workstream.\n\n## Implementation progress (2026-11-21)\n\n- `/batch` uses operation specifications and the existing `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`; it defaults to sequential execution. Parallel admission requires isolated worktrees and, as of PA-1 resource-admission work, explicit per-operation CPU/memory reservations. This adds no second scheduler or persisted workflow store.\n- `/batch` is wired into the sidecar slash router and REPL, so the TUI obtains its completion/help entry from the same slash catalogue. The implemented actions are `run`, `validate`, `status`, `list`, `pause`, `resume`, `cancel`, and `results`. Pause writes the durable `PAUSED` status at a safe boundary. `/loop` and `/goal` lifecycles remain unimplemented.\n- Regression tests cover quoting/Unicode, workspace and symlink boundaries, sequential execution, durable state, partial aggregation, CPU/memory admission and legacy serial fallback. The PA-1 slice passed 44 focused operation/batch tests, 28 operation release/review tests, and the CLI TypeScript build in its isolated worktree. These are targeted results; they do not replace the prescribed full-suite qualification. No provider code changed.\n- A definition-first XERJ lookup in the `opencode` corpus found `packages/schema/src/goal.ts:11` (MIT) with bounded round count, stall count, and boot identity fields. This informed the roadmap comparison only; no peer code was copied. The corpus is flagged as incompletely indexed, and the listed `goose` corpus was unavailable.\n- This is an implementation checkpoint, not completion evidence for PA-4. Authority enforcement, prompt-only runtime compatibility tests, distinct pause semantics, `/loop`, `/goal`, full batch terminal-state persistence, adversarial coverage, interface-level behavior tests, minimal-provider workflow fixtures, independent review, and comparative evaluation remain outstanding.\n# PA-4 transient delegation authority milestone\n\nThis milestone narrows the existing in-turn `spawn_subagent` path. It does not introduce another scheduler or durable store.\n\n## Contract\n\n- The compatible prompt-only call selects `semantic_explorer`, a predefined read-only profile.\n- Optional `profile` selects only an existing role; optional `inputs` accepts string values and conveys task data, never authority.\n- Unknown fields, unknown profiles, non-string inputs, and oversized requests are rejected before child construction. The legacy `max_steps` field remains accepted but is ignored and never changes host limits.\n- Child tools are the intersection of the parent's currently offered tools, explicit matching parent grants (when configured), and the selected profile's allowlist. The dispatcher repeats this exact-name check before any tool effect.\n- A child has `fullAccess: false`; a writable profile requires an already established isolated worktree. Delegation depth is limited to one child level and each parent admits at most four child attempts.\n- Active children, delegated inference steps, and execution duration are bounded. Defaults are four active children, 64 delegated inference requests per parent turn, and two minutes per child.\n- Operators may set `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_CHILDREN` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_ACTIVE` (1–16), `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_STEPS` (1–256), and `NEXUS_DELEGATION_MAX_DURATION_MS` (1,000–600,000). Invalid values fail closed. Delegation depth is fixed at one level.\n- Completion returns a validated structured receipt. An incomplete child with workspace mutations is `UNKNOWN_EFFECT`; a reported failure is not treated as completion.\n- The child uses its own conversation and returns only a bounded summary receipt to the parent. Parent cancellation continues to signal the child.\n\nThese restrictions preserve prompt-only compatibility while intentionally refusing legacy prompt-only requests that relied on unrestricted writes. The process-wide active-child ceiling is shared by transient delegations; aggregate budgets across separate parent agents and independent evidence review remain open PA-4 work.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ was consulted per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md`. The useful reference was the peer pattern of host-owned predefined role profiles with allowlisted tools. No peer implementation was copied. XERJ field report was submitted after preview; GitHub PR creation was unavailable and the report endpoint accepted the fallback.\n\n## Qualification status\n\nThe shared `SUITE` claim was active during implementation, so no local tests were run for this milestone. TypeScript compilation and hosted CI are required before merge. This document records implementation intent, not test evidence.\n# `/batch` on the operation engine\n\n`/batch` accepts the same JSON work-unit format as `nexus operation run`. It validates the whole dependency graph before starting, then stores and executes it through `OperationStore`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, and `OperationRunner`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"docs-and-tests\",\n  \"objective\": \"Update the guide and verify the examples\",\n  \"units\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"guide\",\n      \"title\": \"Update the guide\",\n      \"role\": \"implementer\",\n      \"prompt\": \"Update the guide examples for the new API\",\n      \"authorizedScope\": [\"docs/guide.md\"],\n      \"executionResources\": { \"cpuMilli\": 1000, \"memoryBytes\": 536870912 }\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"verify\",\n      \"title\": \"Verify the examples\",\n      \"role\": \"tester\",\n      \"prompt\": \"Run the documented examples and report the results\",\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"guide\"],\n      \"executionResources\": { \"cpuMilli\": 1000, \"memoryBytes\": 536870912 }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe accepted fields are the operation fields: `objective`, optional `id`, `units`, optional `resources`, `discoverCatalog`, `policy`, `git`, and `boundaries`. A unit uses `prompt` for its task, optional `role`, `dependsOn`, `requires`, `required`, `taskClass`, `contextBudget`, `inputs`, `authorizedScope`, `unresolvedAssumptions`, and optional `executionResources`. Unknown keys, missing dependencies, and cycles are refused before execution.\n\n## Commands\n\n```text\n/batch <spec-file>\n/batch run <spec-file>\n/batch validate <spec-file>\n/batch status [id]\n/batch list\n/batch pause [id]\n/batch resume [id]\n/batch cancel [id]\n/batch results [id]\n```\n\nPaths may be quoted or backslash-escaped and must resolve to a regular file inside the selected workspace. The file is limited to 256 KiB. `run` is sequential by default. To request parallel units, set `policy.maxConcurrentUnits`, `policy.executionBudget`, `git.isolate: true`, and `executionResources` on every unit. `cpuMilli` is a reservation in thousandths of a CPU core; `memoryBytes` is a byte reservation. The scheduler admits a unit only when its declared reservation fits the operation budget, alongside model/resource eligibility, dependencies, provider slot limits, and serialized verified integration. These declarations are admission estimates supplied by the operator; Nexus does not enforce OS-level CPU or memory quotas and does not report them as measured usage. Without complete reservations or isolated worktrees, new parallel specs are refused, and older persisted parallel records run serially.\n\nWhen an ID is omitted, a single active batch is selected. Multiple active batches require an explicit ID; if none is active, a sole batch is selected only when that is unambiguous. `/batch list` shows the available IDs. `status` shows progress; `results` shows each task's status, summary, and recorded verifier state. A blocked required task prevents `COMPLETE`; aggregate output reports `PARTIAL`, `BLOCKED`, `FAILED`, or `CANCELLED` as derived from the durable operation record.\n\nThe operation scheduler continues independent work while dependencies remain blocked. Retry, provider recovery, and restart use the existing operation decisions and state. Cancellation of an active `/batch run` uses the same signal as `/team`; a separate cancel request refuses an unsettled stored unit until recovery can classify it.\n\n## Current implementation boundary\n\nPause writes a distinct `PAUSED` status into the durable operation record under the operation driver lock. If a work unit is currently executing, pause returns a safe-boundary message; retry once the unit settles. Resume continues from the preserved record. Schema v4 adds explicit execution reservations; older records migrate additively and missing reservations clamp parallel scheduling to one. `/loop`, `/goal`, independent acceptance review, and minimal-provider end-to-end fixtures are not supplied by this batch slice.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20014-b98e-7f50-9b44-1c38f2a1201a","create_time":1795297713.627713}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":15900,"sampled_through_sequence":15897,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
